###Pure
// legal-core -- the master agreements two parties trade under, and the terms that decide what
// happens when one of them defaults.
//
// The shape worth stating once, because every class below is a consequence of it: a master
// agreement is not a document, it is a SINGLE AGREEMENT between exactly two legal persons.
// The ISDA Master, its Schedule, every Confirmation under it and every Credit Support Annex
// are one contract, which is the entire reason close-out netting works: on default there is
// one net sum owed, not one claim per trade. So the parties are modelled as rows keyed by the
// ROLE they sign in -- Party A, Party B, the guarantor, the credit support provider -- and
// each of those rows points at a core_party::LegalEntity, because who signed is a party-master
// fact and this project has no business restating it.
//
// Layer 1: depends on core-party and on nothing else.

// The governance vocabulary for legal documentation. Separate from any upstream profile
// because the question a legal reviewer asks is not the question a data owner asks: whether
// the wording has been through counsel, and whether it still matches the house template.
Profile legal_core::LglControlled
{
   stereotypes: [ legallyReviewed, templateDeviation, negotiated ];
   tags: [ reviewedBy, reviewDate, opinionRef, deviationTicket ];
}


// The umbrella contract between two parties -- an ISDA Master, a GMRA, a GMSLA -- under which
// individual transactions are struck.
//
// Stereotyped and tagged because this is the class every downstream exposure and netting
// calculation hangs off, and a change to it is a change to somebody's regulatory capital.
Class <<legal_core::LglControlled.legallyReviewed>>
      {legal_core::LglControlled.reviewedBy = 'legal-ops',
       legal_core::LglControlled.reviewDate = '2025-03-31'}
      legal_core::MasterAgreement
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   typeCode: String[1];
   lawCode: String[1];
   // What the desk calls it. Not a key: the same reference is reused across a group's
   // subsidiaries often enough that it identifies nothing.
   agreementReference: String[1];
   executionDate: StrictDate[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // Set when the agreement is terminated, never cleared. Trades under a terminated master
   // survive it, and so must the row.
   terminationDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   terminationCurrency: String[1];
   documentStatus: String[1];
   // Multibranch means Party A may act through any of its listed branches and the netting
   // opinion has to cover every one of them, so it is a term of the agreement rather than a
   // booking detail.
   isMultibranch: Boolean[1];
   isUmbrella: Boolean[1];

   // In force until terminated. The absence of the date is the test, not the row's presence.
   isLive() { $this.terminationDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// A published master form -- ISDA 2002, ISDA 1992 Multicurrency Cross-Border, GMRA 2011,
// GMSLA 2010 -- and what it is architecturally.
//
// Kept as a class rather than an enum because a netting opinion is written per form, and an
// enum cannot carry the publisher and the year that the opinion is scoped to.
Class legal_core::AgreementType
{
   typeCode: String[1];
   formName: String[1];
   // ISDA, ICMA/SIFMA, ISLA. The trade association that owns the wording.
   publisher: String[1];
   publicationYear: Integer[1];
   productScope: String[1];
   // A master form; false for the annexes and confirmations that hang off one.
   isMasterForm: Boolean[1];
   // Whether the form contains the single-agreement clause, which is the clause the whole of
   // close-out netting depends on.
   hasSingleAgreementClause: Boolean[1];
}

// The law the agreement is construed under, and the courts that hear a dispute about it.
//
// Not the same question as where either party is incorporated: an English-law ISDA between a
// German bank and a Japanese fund is the ordinary case, and the enforceability analysis then
// spans three legal systems.
Class legal_core::GoverningLaw
{
   lawCode: String[1];
   lawName: String[1];
   // Exclusive or non-exclusive submission, and to whose courts. Prose rather than a code
   // because the clause is prose and flattening it loses the exclusivity.
   forumClause: String[1];
   isCommonLaw: Boolean[1];
   // Whether the courts here recognise close-out netting as a matter of law, before any
   // opinion is commissioned for a particular counterparty type.
   recognisesCloseOutNetting: Boolean[1];
}

// One legal entity's role in one agreement: who is Party A, who is Party B, who guarantees.
//
// Keyed (agreement, role) rather than (agreement, entity), because the interesting question
// is always "who is Party B on this master" and because the same entity can appear twice --
// as Party A and as its affiliate's credit support provider -- without the row colliding.
// This is the class that reaches core-party.
Class legal_core::AgreementParty
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   partyRole: String[1];
   entityId: String[1];
   // The branch the party books through, meaningful only on a multibranch master.
   bookingBranch: String[0..1];
   isCalculationAgent: Boolean[1];
   signedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   signatoryName: String[0..1];
   noticeAddressRef: String[0..1];
}

// A Credit Support Annex or Deed: the collateral terms attached to one master.
//
// Keyed (agreement, annexCode) because one master routinely carries several -- a variation
// margin CSA and a regulatory initial margin CSA/CSD are different documents with different
// thresholds, different custodians and different transfer mechanics, and collapsing them to
// one annex per master is how an IM call gets netted against a VM call that was never
// nettable.
Class <<legal_core::LglControlled.negotiated>>
      {legal_core::LglControlled.reviewedBy = 'collateral-legal'}
      legal_core::CreditSupportAnnex
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   annexCode: String[1];
   annexForm: String[1];
   // VM or IM. The regulatory regime differs, and so does whether the collateral may be
   // rehypothecated at all.
   marginType: String[1];
   // Title transfer (English law CSA) versus security interest (New York law CSA). The single
   // most consequential term in the document: it decides whether posted collateral is the
   // taker's property or the poster's, and therefore what happens to it in an insolvency.
   isTitleTransfer: Boolean[1];
   baseCurrency: String[1];
   // Unsecured exposure the taker tolerates before calling. Null is not zero: null means the
   // term was left to the Schedule, zero means it was negotiated to nil.
   threshold: Float[0..1];
   minimumTransferAmount: Float[0..1];
   roundingAmount: Float[0..1];
   independentAmount: Float[0..1];
   valuationAgent: String[0..1];
   notificationTime: String[0..1];
   // In scope of the uncleared margin rules, which makes the terms mandatory rather than
   // negotiated.
   isRegulatoryMargin: Boolean[1];

   hasThreshold() { $this.threshold->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// One asset an annex accepts as collateral, and at what valuation.
//
// Keyed by all three of (agreement, annex, asset): the same government bond is eligible at
// 98% under the VM annex and ineligible under the IM annex of the SAME master, so neither the
// annex nor the asset can be dropped from the key.
Class legal_core::EligibleCollateral
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   annexCode: String[1];
   assetCode: String[1];
   assetDescription: String[1];
   // The percentage of market value the collateral counts for. The haircut is the complement,
   // and is derived rather than stored so the two can never disagree.
   valuationPercentage: Float[1];
   collateralCurrency: String[1];
   isCashEquivalent: Boolean[1];
   minimumRating: String[0..1];
   maximumResidualMaturityYears: Integer[0..1];
   concentrationLimit: Float[0..1];

   haircutPercentage() { 100.0 - $this.valuationPercentage } : Float[1];
}

// A law firm's opinion that close-out netting bites in one jurisdiction, for one master form,
// against one type of counterparty.
//
// All three are part of the key and none is droppable: an opinion covering a bank in Germany
// says nothing about an insurer in Germany, and a Delaware opinion on the 2002 ISDA does not
// carry over to a GMRA. Capital relief for netting is only available where an opinion covers
// the exact triple, which is why this table is read as a lookup and not as documentation.
Class <<legal_core::LglControlled.legallyReviewed>>
      {legal_core::LglControlled.opinionRef = 'NET-OPINIONS-2025',
       legal_core::LglControlled.reviewDate = '2025-01-31'}
      legal_core::NettingOpinion
{
   jurisdictionCode: String[1];
   typeCode: String[1];
   // BANK, INSURER, FUND, CORPORATE, SOVEREIGN, PENSION. The insolvency regime that applies
   // to the counterparty, which is what the opinion is actually about.
   counterpartyType: String[1];
   opinionProvider: String[1];
   opinionDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Clean means unqualified. A qualified opinion is not a netting opinion for capital
   // purposes, however encouraging its wording.
   isCleanOpinion: Boolean[1];
   supportsCloseOutNetting: Boolean[1];
   // Enforceability of the collateral arrangement is a SEPARATE question from netting of the
   // trades, and is opined on separately.
   supportsCollateralEnforceability: Boolean[1];
   lastReviewedDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   qualification: String[0..1];

   isQualified() { $this.qualification->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// How the close-out amount is computed when an event of default is called.
//
// One per master, which is why it is keyed by the agreement alone. Split out from
// MasterAgreement rather than folded into it because these are Schedule elections -- they are
// negotiated separately, amended separately, and a master can exist while they are still open.
Class legal_core::CloseOutTerms
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   // MARKET_QUOTATION, LOSS (1992 forms) or CLOSE_OUT_AMOUNT (2002). Determines whether the
   // determining party polls dealers or may use its own commercially reasonable model.
   paymentMeasure: String[1];
   // First Method or Second Method. First Method lets the defaulting party's in-the-money
   // position be walked away from; almost nobody elects it, and knowing that requires the row.
   payerBasis: String[1];
   // Automatic Early Termination -- close-out happens by operation of the contract without a
   // notice, elected where a local insolvency regime would otherwise freeze the right to
   // terminate.
   automaticEarlyTermination: Boolean[1];
   setOffElected: Boolean[1];
   terminationCurrency: String[1];
   gracePeriodDays: Integer[1];
   calculationAgentRole: String[0..1];

   usesCloseOutAmount() { $this.paymentMeasure == 'CLOSE_OUT_AMOUNT' } : Boolean[1];
}

// A negotiated Schedule election: cross-default threshold, specified entities, additional
// termination events.
//
// Keyed (agreement, election): the elections are the agreement. Two masters on the same
// published form differ only here, and a downstream comparison of terms across a portfolio is
// a comparison of these rows.
Class legal_core::ElectionTerm
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   electionCode: String[1];
   electionName: String[1];
   // Stereotyped at the PROPERTY level: the value is the bit that was bargained over, and a
   // reviewer diffing two agreements looks at this column and no other.
   <<legal_core::LglControlled.negotiated>> electedValue: String[1];
   thresholdAmount: Float[0..1];
   thresholdCurrency: String[0..1];
   // An election can be asymmetric -- a threshold that applies to one party only is the normal
   // outcome between a dealer and a fund.
   appliesToPartyRole: String[0..1];
   scheduleReference: String[0..1];
}

// A change to an executed agreement, in force from its own effective date.
//
// Keyed (agreement, number) and never overwritten: what the terms were on a past date is the
// question an unwind or a dispute asks, and an amendment applied in place cannot answer it.
Class legal_core::Amendment
{
   agreementId: String[1];
   amendmentNumber: Integer[1];
   // BILATERAL_AMENDMENT, AMENDED_AND_RESTATED, PROTOCOL_UPTAKE. Protocol uptake is here as
   // well as in ProtocolAdherence because the protocol amends the agreement, and the agreement
   // has to show that it was amended.
   amendmentType: String[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];
   amendedSection: String[0..1];
   description: String[0..1];
   // Marks the amendment as moving the document off the house template, which is what the
   // templateDeviation stereotype exists to flag at the model level.
   <<legal_core::LglControlled.templateDeviation>> isTemplateDeviation: Boolean[1];
   executedBy: String[0..1];
}

// A published multilateral protocol -- 2013 EMIR Portfolio Reconciliation, 2020 IBOR Fallbacks,
// the Stay Protocols.
//
// The mechanism matters: a protocol amends every agreement between any two adhering parties at
// once, without either of them signing anything bilateral. So it is modelled as its own
// element and the amendment it makes is derived from the pair of adherences, not from a row
// per agreement.
Class legal_core::Protocol
{
   protocolCode: String[1];
   protocolName: String[1];
   publisher: String[1];
   publicationDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Some protocols close to new adherents; most do not.
   adherenceCutoffDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   subjectMatter: String[1];
   isMultilateral: Boolean[1];
}

// One entity's adherence to one protocol. The second class that reaches core-party.
//
// Keyed (protocol, entity), and adherence is by LEGAL ENTITY rather than by agreement -- that
// is the whole point of a protocol. Revocation is recorded rather than deleted, because
// agreements amended while the adherence was live stay amended.
Class legal_core::ProtocolAdherence
{
   protocolCode: String[1];
   entityId: String[1];
   adherenceDate: StrictDate[1];
   adherenceLetterRef: String[0..1];
   // Adhering as principal, as agent for funds, or both. An asset manager's adherence letter
   // covering 400 funds is one row here and 400 agreements amended downstream.
   adheringCapacity: String[0..1];
   isRevoked: Boolean[1];
   revocationDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   isEffective() { $this.revocationDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------- associations
// Navigation only. Two of these cross the project boundary into core-party, which is the
// point of this layer: the agreement knows who signed it without holding a copy of the party
// master.

// An agreement to its published form.
Association legal_core::LglAgreementForm
{
   agreementType: legal_core::AgreementType[1];
   agreementsOfType: legal_core::MasterAgreement[*];
}

Association legal_core::LglAgreementLaw
{
   governingLaw: legal_core::GoverningLaw[1];
   agreementsUnderLaw: legal_core::MasterAgreement[*];
}

// The two (or more) signatories. To-many rather than two named ends, because guarantors and
// credit support providers are parties to the same document and a pair of ends cannot hold
// them.
Association legal_core::LglAgreementParties
{
   partyAgreement: legal_core::MasterAgreement[1];
   parties: legal_core::AgreementParty[*];
}

// CROSSES INTO core-party. The role row points at the registered legal person, so "every
// master this entity is Party B on" is one hop from the party master and the entity's legal
// name is never copied into this project.
Association legal_core::LglPartyEntity
{
   partyEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   agreementRoles: legal_core::AgreementParty[*];
}

Association legal_core::LglAgreementAnnexes
{
   annexAgreement: legal_core::MasterAgreement[1];
   creditSupportAnnexes: legal_core::CreditSupportAnnex[*];
}

// Reached by a TWO-column join, because an annex is only identified together with its master.
Association legal_core::LglAnnexCollateral
{
   collateralAnnex: legal_core::CreditSupportAnnex[1];
   eligibleCollateral: legal_core::EligibleCollateral[*];
}

// [0..1] on the terms side: a master can be executed with its close-out elections still open.
Association legal_core::LglAgreementCloseOut
{
   closeOutAgreement: legal_core::MasterAgreement[1];
   closeOutTerms: legal_core::CloseOutTerms[0..1];
}

Association legal_core::LglAgreementElections
{
   electedAgreement: legal_core::MasterAgreement[1];
   elections: legal_core::ElectionTerm[*];
}

Association legal_core::LglAgreementAmendments
{
   amendedAgreement: legal_core::MasterAgreement[1];
   amendments: legal_core::Amendment[*];
}

Association legal_core::LglProtocolAdherences
{
   adheredProtocol: legal_core::Protocol[1];
   adherences: legal_core::ProtocolAdherence[*];
}

// CROSSES INTO core-party. Adherence is an attribute of the entity, not of any one agreement,
// so this end is what lets a downstream project ask whether both sides of a master have
// adhered -- which is the test for whether the protocol amended it.
Association legal_core::LglAdherentEntity
{
   adherentEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   protocolAdherences: legal_core::ProtocolAdherence[*];
}

// An opinion is written about a form, so the form is one leg of the lookup.
Association legal_core::LglTypeOpinions
{
   opinionAgreementType: legal_core::AgreementType[1];
   nettingOpinions: legal_core::NettingOpinion[*];
}

// CROSSES INTO core-party, to Jurisdiction rather than to LegalEntity: an opinion is about a
// legal system and its insolvency regime, and core-party's Jurisdiction is already that
// concept -- Delaware and Jersey included, which is exactly the granularity an opinion needs.
Association legal_core::LglOpinionJurisdiction
{
   opinionJurisdiction: core_party::Jurisdiction[1];
   jurisdictionOpinions: legal_core::NettingOpinion[*];
}
